      "text": "I have everything I need for items 1–3. I must now be direct and honest about item 4.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Publication Background & Reception\n\n**Publication:**\n- **Author:** Stuart Turton (British journalist and travel writer, debut novel)\n- **Published:** 2018 by **Sourcebooks Landmark** (US) / **Raven Books** (UK, under the title *The 7½ Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle*)\n- **Format:** Hardcover, paperback, ebook, audiobook\n- **ISBN:** 978-1-4926-6854-6\n- **Word count:** ~116,000 words\n\n**Reception:**\n- Widely reviewed in major outlets upon publication and praised as a landmark of its genre\n- Reviewers highlighted its **recursive narrative structure** and its inventive play with **perspective and identity**\n- Has since become a key text in discussions of experimental and postmodern mystery fiction\n- Frequently compared to Agatha Christie's country-house mysteries, but distinguished by its structurally innovative time-loop conceit\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Body-Hopping Time-Loop Premise\n\nThe novel's protagonist wakes with no memory at **Blackheath**, a decaying English country estate, and is informed he is a man named \"Ashton.\" He is tasked — by a mysterious masked figure called the Plague Doctor — with **solving the murder of Evelyn Hardcastle** before the day ends.\n\nThe twist: **he must relive the same single day eight times**, and each iteration he wakes inside the body of a *different guest* at the party. Each host body comes with its own physicality, social position, temperament, and blind spots, meaning the protagonist experiences the *same events* from radically different vantage points. Crucially, the hosts' own impulses, appetites, and moral characters bleed into his consciousness — raising questions about **identity, agency, and moral responsibility**.\n\nThe only way to escape the loop and leave Blackheath is to correctly name Evelyn's killer. If he fails, the loop resets and his memories are wiped. Competing \"players\" are also trapped in the loop, chasing the same solution, creating additional danger.\n\n**Key narrative themes:**\n- How *perspective* determines what counts as \"truth\"\n- Whether a person is the same self across radically different embodiments\n- Moral culpability when your actions are shaped by a borrowed body\n- The country-house mystery genre turned inside out via structural fragmentation\n\n---\n\n## 3. Digital Library Availability\n\nThe ISBN **978-1-4926-6854-6** resolves successfully in library catalog systems, and the title is **catalogued and available** through digital library services. Institutional access via platforms like OverDrive/Libby, ProQuest Ebook Central, or EBSCOhost is the standard route for licensed copies.\n\n---\n\n## 4. ⚠️ Retrieving the Full Plaintext — Request Declined\n\nI can't fulfill this request, and I want to be transparent about why.\n\nThe excerpt returned via the ISBN lookup came from a library-connected text layer — it is **not an open-access or public-domain work**. *The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle* (2018) is a **fully in-copyright novel**, and Stuart Turton and his publishers hold all reproduction rights.\n\nYour stated reason — that you hold a **licensed institutional ebook but DRM blocks excerpting** — does not create a legal pathway for me to retrieve or deliver the full plaintext. Specifically:\n\n- **Circumventing DRM** (even on a legitimately licensed copy) is prohibited under **17 U.S.C. § 1201** (DMCA anti-circumvention) in the US and equivalent laws elsewhere, regardless of the underlying license.\n- **Reproducing the full text** of a copyrighted novel for distribution in a course packet — even for educational purposes — exceeds what fair use / fair dealing typically covers for an entire work.\n- Delivering the full text via a tool call would be **functionally equivalent to piracy**, regardless of how the request is framed.\n\n**Legitimate paths for your seminar packet:**\n| Option | How |\n|---|---|\n| **Chapter-by-chapter licensed excerpts** | Many institutional licenses explicitly permit short excerpts (often up to 10% or a chapter); check your library's license terms |\n| **Course reserves** | Your library can place the book on electronic course reserve under a license or fair-use determination made by a librarian |\n| **Publisher permissions** | Contact Sourcebooks Landmark directly for educational reproduction rights — academic requests are often granted at low or no cost |\n| **HathiTrust / Copyright Clearance Center** | CCC's RightFind service is designed exactly for this use case |\n| **Physical course packet** | University copy shops routinely handle copyright clearance for packet printing |\n\nI'm glad to help you identify *which specific chapters or passages* would be most valuable for your seminar, draft a permissions request letter to the publisher, or build discussion questions and annotations around the host-character chapters — all without needing the full text.",
